<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battles of Khalkhin Gol</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Nomonhan Incident · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-japanese-border-conflicts">Soviet–Japanese border conflicts</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battles of Khalkhin Gol, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> as the Nomonhan Incident, were fought from May to September 1939 on the Khalkhin Gol river on the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> and Japanese-controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchukuo">Manchukuo</a>. Soviet and Mongolian forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgy-zhukov">Georgy Zhukov</a> encircled and destroyed much of the Japanese 23rd Division in August. The defeat turned Japan away from war with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, and the two countries signed a neutrality pact in 1941.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battles-of-khalkhin-gol.jpg" alt="Battles of Khalkhin Gol" width="320" height="206" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dōmei Tsushin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_soldiers_creeping_in_front_of_wrecked_Soviet_tanks.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the Japanese occupation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> in 1931, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> turned its military interests to the Soviet territories bordering it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the People's Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> signed a Mutual Assistance Pact in March 1936, allowing Soviet troops into Mongolia, and Japan signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-comintern-pact">Anti-Comintern Pact</a> in response. After Japan's full invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in July 1937 the Soviet Union sent the 57th Special Corps under Ivan Konev to Mongolia. The first major Soviet-Japanese border incident, the Battle of Lake Khasan, took place in 1938 in Primorye, and clashes along the border of Manchuria were frequent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The fighting began on 11 May 1939, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> cavalry drove back a Mongolian unit of 70 to 90 men that had entered the disputed area in search of grazing. On 28 May Soviet and Mongolian forces surrounded and destroyed a Japanese force under Lieutenant Colonel Yaozo Azuma, which lost 63 per cent of its men. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgy-zhukov">Georgy Zhukov</a> arrived on 5 June as corps commander with more motorised and armoured forces. On 27 June the Japanese 2nd Air Brigade struck the Soviet airbase at Tamsak-Bulak, on an order from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> that Tokyo had not approved.</p><p>Fearing war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, Zhukov planned an offensive to end the fighting, and on 20 August 1939 attacked with a force supplied by at least 4,000 trucks from Chita, 600 km away. It had around 498 BT-5 and BT-7 tanks and over 550 fighters and bombers, with two Mongolian cavalry divisions. The offensive was a simultaneous three-pronged attack by aircraft, tanks and infantry, the first of its kind in modern warfare, and similar tactics were later used in Operation Uranus at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Japanese records report about 20,000 casualties, 162 aircraft lost in combat and 42 tanks disabled. The Soviets at first claimed 29,085 Japanese casualties and later raised the figure to 61,000.</p><p>The battle earned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kwantung-army">Kwantung Army</a> the displeasure of officials in Tokyo, chiefly because it had started and escalated the fighting without government authorisation. With Chinese resistance and the German-Soviet non-aggression pact, the defeat moved the Imperial General Staff away from the North Strike policy of seizing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a> as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-baikal">Lake Baikal</a>. Japan favoured the Nanshin-ron, or strike south, strategy, and the Soviet-Japanese Neutrality Pact followed in April 1941. Zhukov earned his first Hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> award, and Japan raised tank production from 500 a year to 1,200 in 1939.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battles of Khalkhin Gol” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…launched the invasion of the rest of China after the Marco Polo Bridge incident of 1937, and in May 1939 a clash between Manchukuoan and Mongolian cavalry grew into the <b>Battles of Khalkhin Gol</b>, in which a combined Soviet–Mongolian force defeated the Kwantung Army with limited Manchukuoan support.</blockquote><a href="/empire/manchukuo">Manchukuo · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Soviet and Mongolian forces defeated Japan at <b>Khalkhin Gol</b> in 1939.</blockquote><a href="/state/mongolian-peoples-republic">Mongolian People's Republic</a></li></ul></section>
